# The ledger of `vectors`: what a program can do with vectors of floats
# compared and searched for the nearest, one block per capability, and what
# Rebel answers.
#
# Neither Go's nor Rust's standard library holds vectors of floats or a
# search for the nearest, so their lines read `-`; Python's holds three
# functions over coordinates (`math.dist`, `math.hypot`, `math.sumprod`). The
# task (the author, 2026-11-17) named the yardsticks: hnswlib, faiss and
# pgvector's operators. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-19: `dir()` of
# `hnswlib` 0.8.0 and of its `Index`, built from its source into the scratch
# folder since no wheel exists for Python 3.15; `dir()` of `faiss` 1.16.0
# (faiss-cpu), of its `IndexHNSWFlat` and its `hnsw`, and its names holding
# `Flat`, `HNSW`, `METRIC`, `knn`, `L2`, `index`; pgvector's operators and
# functions from its documentation (pgvector 0.8), not installed here, so its
# lines are read and never run.
#
# A vector is `[f32]`, as all three yardsticks hold it; matrices and arrays of
# more dimensions stay out (the task).
#
# The differential, 2026-11-19, on 10,000 vectors of 32 components drawn
# uniformly from -1 to 1 by `random`'s PCG seeded (1, 2), 200 queries drawn
# after them, k 10, 16 links, 200 candidates at construction, every library
# on one thread. Exact search: `nearest` answered numpy's float64 ids, ties by
# index, on 200 of 200 queries under the euclidean metric and 200 of 200
# under the cosine. Recall of the k found against those ids, at ef 10, 50,
# 100 and 200: euclidean, Rebel 0.5890, 0.9465, 0.9910, 0.9995; hnswlib
# 0.5900, 0.9445, 0.9915, 0.9995; faiss's `IndexHNSWFlat` 0.6160, 0.9485,
# 0.9905, 0.9995. Cosine, Rebel 0.6035, 0.9485, 0.9885, 0.9995; hnswlib
# 0.6030, 0.9465, 0.9875, 0.9995; faiss on normalized vectors 0.6460, 0.9635,
# 0.9955, 1.0000. Cost on this Mac at -O2: the index built in 5.4 s, where
# hnswlib took 2.5 s and faiss 0.8 s; a query at ef 100 about 100
# microseconds, hnswlib's 46; 21 MB resident at most; written as 2,743,300
# bytes and read back as the same graph.

capability vector/dot  the dot product of two vectors
  heroes  vectors.dot
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  math.sumprod, faiss.fvec_inner_product, hnswlib.Index (space "ip")
  pgvector  inner_product, <#>
  note    each product exact in f64 and the sum rounded once to f32: Python's `sum` over the f32 products in f64 gives the same before the last rounding (observed). faiss and pgvector sum in f32 and may differ in the last bits

capability vector/norm  the length of a vector
  heroes  vectors.norm
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  math.hypot, faiss.fvec_norms_L2sqr
  pgvector  vector_norm
  note    the squares summed in f64, one root, one rounding to f32; `math.hypot` scales to avoid overflow, which f64 sums of f32 squares never need

capability vector/cosine  the cosine similarity and distance of two vectors
  heroes  vectors.cosine_similarity, vectors.cosine_distance
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index (space "cosine")
  pgvector  cosine_distance, <=>
  note    the dot product over the root of the product of the squared lengths, as pgvector divides (one rounding where two roots take three), held within -1 and 1. A vector of zeros fails `invalid`, where pgvector answers NaN and hnswlib's normalization leaves it zero

capability vector/euclidean  the euclidean distance of two vectors, and its square
  heroes  vectors.euclidean_distance, vectors.squared_euclidean_distance
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  math.dist, faiss.fvec_L2sqr, faiss.METRIC_L2, hnswlib.Index (space "l2")
  pgvector  l2_distance, <->
  note    pgvector's `<->` is the distance and faiss's and hnswlib's `l2` its square; both are offered

capability vector/manhattan  the sum of the absolute differences of two vectors
  heroes  vectors.manhattan_distance
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  faiss.METRIC_L1
  pgvector  l1_distance, <+>

capability vector/metric  a distance chosen by a value, and one query's distances to many vectors
  heroes  vectors.Metric, vectors.distance, vectors.distances
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  faiss.pairwise_distances, faiss.METRIC_INNER_PRODUCT
  pgvector  -
  note    the inner product orders as pgvector's `<#>`, the product negated so the nearest is the smallest; faiss's `METRIC_INNER_PRODUCT` answers the product itself, the largest first. `pairwise_distances` takes many queries; a program loops over them

capability vector/normalize  a vector scaled to length one
  heroes  vectors.normalize
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  faiss.normalize_L2, faiss.fvec_renorm_L2
  pgvector  l2_normalize
  note    each quotient in f64, rounded once; a vector of zeros, or one holding a nan or an infinity, fails `invalid`, where faiss and pgvector leave zeros as they are

capability vector/arithmetic  two vectors added, subtracted, multiplied element by element, a vector scaled
  heroes  vectors.add, vectors.subtract, vectors.multiply, vectors.scale
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  faiss.fvec_madd
  pgvector  +, -, *
  note    faiss's `fvec_madd`, a plus b times a factor, is `add(a: a, b: scale(b, by: f))`

capability vector/aggregate  the sum and the mean of many vectors
  heroes  vectors.sum, vectors.mean
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  pgvector  sum, avg
  note    each component summed in f64 and rounded once; the mean of 0.1, 0.2 and 0.4 as f32 is numpy's float64 mean rounded to f32 (observed)

capability vector/dimensions  how many components a vector has
  heroes  built-in len
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  faiss.Index.d, hnswlib.Index.dim
  pgvector  vector_dims
  note    an index's is `vectors.dimension_of`

capability vector/subvector  the components of a vector from one place to another
  heroes  built-in slice
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  pgvector  subvector

capability vector/half-precision  a vector held at half precision
  heroes  math.f32_to_f16_bits, math.f16_bits_to_f32
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  pgvector  halfvec
  note    `numbers` converts each component; an index of half-precision vectors is not offered, the vectors are held as f32

capability vector/binary  a vector reduced to one bit a component, and the distances of bit vectors
  heroes  vectors.quantize_binary, vectors.hamming_distance, vectors.jaccard_distance
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  faiss.IndexBinaryFlat, faiss.IndexBinaryHNSW
  pgvector  binary_quantize, bit, hamming_distance, jaccard_distance, <~>, <%>
  note    a bit set where a component is above zero, the first in the highest bit, as pgvector writes a bit string; a nan gives a clear bit. Two bit vectors with no bit set are at a Jaccard distance of 0.0, as SciPy 1.18.1's `jaccard` answers (observed). The nearest bit vectors are found by `hamming_distance` in a loop; an index of them is not offered

capability vector/sparse  a vector held as its components that are not zero
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  pgvector  sparsevec
  note    a vector of indices and values with the same distances; not yet written

capability nearest/exact  the k nearest of a list of vectors, by exact search
  heroes  vectors.nearest, vectors.Neighbor
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  faiss.IndexFlat, faiss.IndexFlatL2, faiss.IndexFlatIP, faiss.knn, faiss.knn_L2sqr, faiss.knn_inner_product, hnswlib.BFIndex
  pgvector  ORDER BY <-> LIMIT k without an index
  note    equal to numpy's float64 ids on 400 queries of the differential; ties by index. A nan or an infinity fails `invalid`, since ordering a nan aborts

capability nearest/range  every vector within a distance, by exact search
  heroes  vectors.within
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  faiss.IndexFlat.range_search
  pgvector  WHERE <-> < r
  note    the radius is compared with the distance as answered, in f32, and is inclusive; faiss's is exclusive and in its squared measure

capability hnsw/new  an HNSW index made empty, with its links, its search at construction and its seed
  heroes  vectors.new_index, vectors.Options, vectors.default_options, vectors.Index
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index, hnswlib.Index.init_index, faiss.IndexHNSWFlat, faiss.HNSW.efConstruction
  pgvector  CREATE INDEX USING hnsw WITH (m, ef_construction)
  note    hnswlib's defaults, 16 links and 200 candidates, seed 100. The level of a vector is drawn with integers alone, so one seed builds one index on every platform; hnswlib draws it with a logarithm. No `max_elements`: the lists grow

capability hnsw/insert  a vector added to the index under an id
  heroes  vectors.insert
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index.add_items, faiss.Index.add, faiss.Index.add_with_ids
  pgvector  INSERT
  note    the id is the program's, any i64, as hnswlib's labels; an id held fails `exists`, where hnswlib's `add_items` replaces its vector. Removed vectors are linked to as any other, so a vector inserted among them is reached; hnswlib leaves them out of an insert's links

capability hnsw/search  the k nearest found by the index, with the breadth of its search
  heroes  vectors.search
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index.knn_query, hnswlib.Index.set_ef, hnswlib.Index.ef, faiss.Index.search, faiss.HNSW.efSearch
  pgvector  hnsw.ef_search
  note    the walk is hnswlib's, and so is its recall in the differential; ef is given at each search instead of held by the index. An ef below k is taken as k, as hnswlib takes it

capability hnsw/filter  the k nearest found by the index among the ids a function accepts
  heroes  vectors.search_where
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index.knn_query (filter), faiss.SearchParametersHNSW, faiss.IDSelector
  pgvector  WHERE beside ORDER BY, hnsw.iterative_scan
  note    the function is called once for each vector the walk reaches, as hnswlib's filter

capability hnsw/delete  an id marked deleted, and the mark taken back
  heroes  vectors.remove, vectors.restore
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index.mark_deleted, hnswlib.Index.unmark_deleted, faiss.Index.remove_ids
  pgvector  DELETE
  note    a removed vector stays a road through the graph, as in hnswlib; faiss's HNSW refuses `remove_ids`

capability hnsw/compact  the index rebuilt without its deleted vectors
  heroes  vectors.compact
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index.init_index (allow_replace_deleted), hnswlib.Index.add_items (replace_deleted)
  pgvector  VACUUM, REINDEX
  note    hnswlib reuses a removed vector's slot for a new one; here the index is built again from what is kept, with its seed

capability hnsw/read  the index's count, ids and vectors read back
  heroes  vectors.size, vectors.contains, vectors.ids_of, vectors.vector_of, vectors.dimension_of, vectors.metric_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index.get_current_count, hnswlib.Index.element_count, hnswlib.Index.get_ids_list, hnswlib.Index.get_items, hnswlib.Index.space, hnswlib.Index.M, hnswlib.Index.ef_construction, hnswlib.Index.get_max_elements, hnswlib.Index.max_elements, faiss.Index.ntotal, faiss.Index.reconstruct, faiss.Index.reconstruct_n, faiss.Index.metric_type
  pgvector  -
  note    under the cosine a vector is answered normalized, as hnswlib's `get_items` answers it

capability hnsw/save  the index written as bytes and read back
  heroes  vectors.to_bytes, vectors.from_bytes
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hnswlib.Index.save_index, hnswlib.Index.load_index, hnswlib.Index.index_file_size, faiss.write_index, faiss.read_index, faiss.serialize_index, faiss.deserialize_index
  pgvector  -
  note    this package's own format; every cut of an index's bytes fails `invalid` and none aborts (tested on every length), and every link is checked to reach its layer before a walk can follow it

